Subject: Portable cycle counter and stack pointer read so the engine builds for arm64

What this does
  - `js_cycles`: the time-stamp counter on x86 (as before); on Windows elsewhere QueryPerformanceCounter, and otherwise CLOCK_MONOTONIC, both scaled to nominal 3 GHz cycles; replaces the 13 direct `__builtin_ia32_rdtsc()` uses (GC admission budget, GC and nursery statistics)
  - the conservative stack scan's stack-pointer read: the x86-64 instruction as before, `mov %0, sp` on aarch64, a local's address elsewhere

Why we need it
  The engine did not compile for aarch64 (Android NDK, Apple): `__builtin_ia32_rdtsc` is x86-only and `mov %%rsp` is x86 assembly. Found by an NDK cross-compile requested to check the JSON and regexp vector paths.

Soundness
  x86 code is unchanged (same builtin, same instruction). The GC admits a collection by comparing cycles spent with elapsed cycles / 16 plus an allowance in cycles; a clock scaled to 3 GHz keeps the ratio exact and the allowance about the same size. The x87 long-double path (0591) and the SSE2 scans (0579, 0588) were already compiled only under `__x86_64__`/`__i386__` with LDBL_MANT_DIG == 64 and `__SSE2__`, with strtod and scalar loops elsewhere. Checked: quickjs.c (-DQJS_REGVM=1 -DQJS_AOT=1, -O2), libregexp.c, libunicode.c and dtoa.c compile with NDK 27.1 aarch64-linux-android24-clang; the x86 build and json_app output are unchanged.

